## totor/1_comment.abas : saying things without running them ##

# a comment starts with one hash and holds everything up to the end of the
# line. abas never looks inside it
print "this line runs"
print "\n"

print "this line has a comment behind it" # and the comment is ignored
print "\n"

# a comment on a line of its own is a note to whoever reads the program, and
# it is the easiest way to switch a line off while you work on it
# print "this line is switched off"

# a comment can open and close on one line, with two hashes at each end
## this whole note is a comment ##
print "this line runs too"
print "\n"

# or it can run on for as many lines as it needs. it ends at the first pair of
# hashes that comes after the one that opened it, so keep the pair that closes
# your note at the end of its last line
## this note runs over
these three lines ##
print "and this line runs as well"
print "\n"

# that first pair is what catches people out. a line that starts with two
# hashes ends the comment right there, and whatever stands behind them is code
# again. put one of these two lines back the way it is and run the file to see
# what abas makes of the tail of it:
# ## this line ends the note above
# "and this half of it is not a comment"

# a comment that is never closed is an error. abas stops with "unterminated
# multi line comment", so the hashes that close a note are not optional
#
# to see that error, take the closing pair off the end of the three line note
# above and run the file again

# nothing of the comments reached abas. to see what abas made of this file, ask
# it for the commands instead of running them
#
#     ./bin/main -p totor/1_comment.abas
#
# to see only the tokens it found
#
#     ./bin/main -t totor/1_comment.abas

exit 0
